  { q: 'Is the raven mentioned in the Avesta?', a: 'The Avesta (the primary sacred text of Zoroastrianism) mentions birds in various contexts, including birds associated with Angra Mainyu (Ahriman, the destructive principle) and birds associated with Ahura Mazda (the wise lord). Corvids are referenced in Zoroastrian literature in connection with the dead and with carrion-eating; the precise taxonomic distinctions (raven vs. crow vs. jackdaw) are not always clear in ancient texts. The Vendidad (a section of the Avesta dealing with purity laws) addresses the disposal of the dead and the birds involved.' },
