    <h1>Ravens in Persian and Zoroastrian Tradition</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In Zoroastrian and Persian tradition, the raven is associated with death, corpse pollution (<em>nasu</em>), and dark augury — but also with prophetic wisdom and divine observation. The raven's real-world role in funerary exposure (Towers of Silence) anchors its religious symbolism, while Persian literary tradition uses the dark corvid as an image of winter, melancholy, and the inevitability of death.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven in Zoroastrian Purity Law</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Zoroastrianism, the ancient Iranian religion founded by the prophet Zarathustra (Zoroaster), maintains strict purity codes governing the management of death and corpses. The Zoroastrian dead may not be buried (which would pollute sacred earth) or cremated (which would pollute sacred fire). Instead, corpses are exposed on the <em>dakhma</em> (Tower of Silence) for consumption by scavenging birds — primarily vultures, but also crows and ravens in regions where these birds are present.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The corpse in Zoroastrian thought carries <em>nasu</em> — a death pollution or demonic impurity that is dangerous and must be neutralised. The birds consuming the flesh simultaneously dispose of the corpse and are themselves associated with this pollution. Ravens (and corvids generally) therefore occupy a distinctive position in Zoroastrian thought: necessary participants in a sacred funerary process, yet associated with the most polluting of substances.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens in the Avesta and Pahlavi Literature</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Avesta's <em>Vendidad</em> (Vīdēvdād), a text concerned with purity laws and the management of death pollution, discusses the proper treatment of corpses and the role of dogs and birds in the exposure ritual. The <em>sag-dīd</em> ritual (the dog's gaze) — in which a dog must view the corpse to ritually counter the nasu demon — is described in detail; birds, including corvids, are referenced as the agents of physical disposal.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>In Pahlavi (Middle Persian) literature of the Sasanian period (3rd–7th centuries CE), birds of evil omen include ravens and crows, which are associated with Ahriman (Angra Mainyu, the destructive spirit) in some contexts. The <em>Bundahishn</em> (a Pahlavi cosmological text) classifies various animals as creations of either the good or evil principle; carrion-eating corvids are sometimes placed among the creations of Ahriman, though this classification is not entirely consistent across texts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven as Omen Bird</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Across Persian and broader Iranian tradition, the raven's call is treated as an omen — most often of separation, travel, or death. The concept of the <em>zāgh</em> (crow/raven) calling as a presage of a loved one's departure or death is a persistent motif in Persian lyric poetry. This omen tradition parallels similar beliefs in Arabic, Turkish, and other neighbouring cultures.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Persian concept of <em>shagūn</em> (augury from bird calls and flights) draws on a wider Indo-Iranian tradition of bird divination. The raven's loud, varied call and its tendency to appear near carcasses made it a natural focus for omen interpretation across the ancient world.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens in Classical Persian Poetry</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Persian classical poetry (the great tradition of Firdawsi, Rumi, Sa'di, Hafiz, and their contemporaries) uses corvid imagery as a symbol of winter, desolation, old age, and the dark side of existence. The raven and crow appear in contrast to the nightingale (symbol of love and spiritual aspiration) and the hoopoe (symbol of spiritual guidance, from Sufi tradition).<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>In Firdawsi's <em>Shahnameh</em> (Book of Kings, c. 1010 CE) — the Persian national epic — birds appear as omens and messengers throughout. The crow (<em>zāgh</em>) calls as a presage of calamity in several episodes. The raven's association with carrion and death gives it a natural role in a text filled with battles and their aftermath.</p>
  </section>

